From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] odb: read_db -backing_file keeps the tables' pages in a
 file
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A database is read into memory a table page at a time, and those pages
can only go to swap: on a large design tens of GB that a later step may
not read for an hour.

read_db -backing_file <file> (dbDatabase::setBackingFile) gives every
table its pages from chunks of its own. A chunk is ordinary memory until
it is paged out, when it is copied to its room in the file, reserved
(posix_fallocate) as the chunk was made so a full disk fails then, and
mapped there. dbDatabase::pageOut and dbBlock::pageOut(dbObjectType)
write the pages of every table, or of one, to the file and free them;
they are read back as they are touched. write_db pages each table out
once it is written, so it holds one table in memory at a time. Without
-backing_file nothing changes. The file is unlinked once open.

Carried in bazel-orfs for OpenROAD#12075, not upstreamed; retires with
the //:bump onto an OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
